  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 PM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A gradual warming trend is expected for the \nremainder of the week with dry conditions continuing.\n\nA positively tilted trough continues to push to our south and east \nthis afternoon while sfc high pressure remains anchored over the \nregion. It is a bit warmer today with temperatures in the mid 50s-\nlower 60s. The surface high will remain in control through the rest \nof the week as upper ridging gradually builds over the area, and a \nclosed low forms near the eastern Gulf Coast. With this, mostly \nsunny skies and a slow warming trend throughout the week are \nexpected. High temperatures will continue to rise by a few degrees \neach day, reaching the upper 60s to 70F Fri and this weekend. \nWidespread temperatures below freezing inland are still expected \ntonight and Wednesday night. Lows will then be in the mid to upper \n30s on Thu/Fri night. Winds remain light this week with the high in \ncontrol.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above average temperatures persist this weekend with \ndry conditions continuing on Saturday. There is a chance for rain on \nSunday, though confidence in rainfall is low (especially inland).\n\nThe upper ridge gradually breaks down by the end of the week, while \nthe Gulf Coast low slowly tracks NE over the weekend. There is still \nnot a whole lot of clarity on the eventual track and strength of the \nsystem. This will determine how much rain we see (and if we see \nanything at all). The deterministic 12z GFS remains an outlier with \na stronger low moving up the coast into the local area, while the \nECMWF and CMC still move a weaker low just offshore. The most likely \ntiming for any precipitation is late Saturday night through Sunday. \nAgain there is quite a bit of uncertainity, with the 50th percentile \nGEFS/EPS ensemble member QPF showing little to no rain area-wide, \nwhile the 75th percentile rainfall shows 0.50-1.00\" across much of \nthe area. The highest amounts will likely occur along the coast with \nthe lowest amounts in the piedmont. Unfortunately, confidence in an \nappreciable wetting rain remains extremely low west of I-95 at this \ntime. Highs remain in the 60s with lows in the 40s.",
